<div id="projRedis" class="grid grid-cols-4 grid-rows-5 gap-4 text-[var(--txt-color)] w-3/5 h-[85vh] justify-center mx-auto my-25 snap-start max-[1050px]:flex max-[1050px]:flex-col">
    <div class="col-span-2 flex items-center justify-center bg-tile-gradient rounded-2xl p-2 move-rd text-2xl font-bold max-[1050px]:order-1 hover:scale-102 duration-700">
        <span class="text-4xl max-[1050px]:text-2xl font-semibold font-titles"><a href="https://github.com/BHalacsy/Redis-Clone" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Redis Clone</a></span>
    </div>
    <div class="col-span-2 row-span-2 col-start-1 row-start-2 flex items-center justify-center bg-tile-gradient rounded-2xl p-4 move-r max-[1050px]:hidden hover:scale-102 duration-700 overflow-y-hidden">
        <img src="/media/icons/skills/redisIcon.svg" alt="Redis Logo" class="w-[50%] h-auto">
    </div>
    <div class="col-span-2 row-span-2 col-start-1 row-start-4 bg-tile-gradient rounded-2xl p-4 move-ru overflow-y-auto max-[1050px]:order-2 hover:scale-102 duration-700">
        <span class="text-lg font-medium block mb-1">Tech used</span>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Written in C++ for high performance using STL containers for efficient in-memory data structures
            without the headache of low level memory management.
            With existing libraries such as <a href="https://www.boost.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Boost.Asio</a>
            for asynchronous networking and <a href="https://github.com/uxlfoundation/oneTBB" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">TBB</a> for fine-grain locking.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Utilized <a href="https://github.com/catchorg/Catch2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Catch2</a> for unit testing commands and expected behaviour.
            Alongside <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">GitHub Actions</a> for CI.
            Developed and ran on Linux (using WSL) to have access to <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/socket.2.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">sockets</a>.
            Later dockerized for mainly for the next <a href="#projPortfolio" class="link-text">project</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="col-span-2 row-span-5 col-start-3 row-start-1 bg-tile-gradient rounded-2xl p-4 move-l overflow-y-auto max-[1050px]:order-3 hover:scale-102 duration-700">
        <span class="text-2xl font-semibold">Description</span>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Redis</a> is an open-source,
            in-memory key value store database known for its high performance and low latency which supports advanced data types such as sets, lists, hashes and more.
            Using for a variety of things but typically as a cache for large scale databases alongside real-time analytics and pub/sub messaging.
            For more check out this <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G1rOthIU-uo" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">brief summary</a> by Fireship
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            I knew that for my very first independent project I wanted to make some existing piece of software and understand its inner workings.
            Initially I considered following one of code crafters' challenges since they provide a bit more hand holding and have automated tests indicating if you complete a subsection of the challenger,
            however seeing how much it would cost for a subscription, I have opted to instead have no hand holding and do the tests myself.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            I began by deciding what language to write the clone in. C++ seemed like an ideal choice as I have had plenty of experience using it, and it is closely related to C in which the original Redis is written in.
            Once that was done I made sure to set up a GitHub Actions workflow to run any existing tests automatically on pushes to the repo.
            Having experience with computer networks came in handy since I had to use linux's sockets, encode and parse commands using RESP and have done multithreaded programming multiple times.
            I began by converting existing code (server and client) done in python to C++ which proved to be more of a challenge than I had thought, but once echo message was set up, it was time to get our hands dirty.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Following this I wanted to follow Redis' RESProtocol for communication as it proved all the utility I had wanted for my clone.
            Initially only allowed to store strings with the basic commands (SET, GET, DEL, EXISTS) I added the first feature of the clone, key expiration.
            I expanded by implementing advanced data structure and their respective commands.
            This had set up the core functionality of the Redis clone, however we were far from done.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Having had worked with multithreading I decided that a thread pool would be fitting for our application to allow concurrent access to the database, this came with its own challenge of handling
            race condition in case multiple threads try to access the same piece of memory. The easy fix for this was to thread lock the kv store in memory when a thread tries to access it, however this
            greatly impacted performance and made accessing the database incredibly slow. I solved this by using TBB's fine-grain locking in which we only lock the currently accessed portion of the kv store
            as opposed to the entire db.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Another feature I knew I wanted to implement was snapshotting as this allowed for the kv store to remain persistent.
            To do this I utilized Boost's serialization which made serializing the kv store much easier as I was using custom data types for managing kv pairs.
            Other features included Pub/Sub messaging, Pipelining, Transaction support for command queueing and LRU (Least Recently Used) key eviction for memory management.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Having all these features I had a pretty deep understanding of Redis core functionality, however I always want to make software which has some sort of real world use.
            I decided to dockerize the Redis clone in hopes of running it as a database for a project down the line. As you can imagine I had some ideas, in fact, it runs the very website you are reading this on.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            For more information check out my GitHub or click the title of this section.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
